INT. LAMPLIGHTERS' GUILD HALL - LONDON - 1872 - EVENING

A long timber room hung with tin ladders and coiled wicks. Rain on the high windows. Five brass lamps burn along a table.

ELIAS THORNBY, fifties, guild master, sits at the head in a velvet waistcoat gone bald at the elbows. AGATHA PRUDHOE, forties, a widow with a ledger, sits at his right. CONSTABLE JOLLIFFE, thirties, damp and unhappy, stands at the door. MRS. QUILL, sixties, the hall's cook, brings a tray. TOM, twelve, the lamplighter's boy, sits cross-legged by the stove.

ELIAS
(to the room)
We are short eleven lamps on Gresham Row.

AGATHA
Twelve. I counted twice.

ELIAS
Then twelve.

JOLLIFFE
Sir, someone's cut them clean. Not smashed. Cut.

AGATHA
(to Elias)
Fuel was drawn on Thursday for sixty lamps. Fifty-eight were lit.

ELIAS
Agatha, not now.

Mrs. Quill sets down the tray. She pours tea for Agatha first, then Elias, then nobody.

MRS. QUILL
(muttering)
Always the sums. Never the stairs.

TOM
Mrs. Quill, what stairs?

MRS. QUILL
Hush, boy.

JOLLIFFE
If this guild cannot keep the street lit, the parish will contract with the gas company.

ELIAS
(to himself)
Gas. The smell of it.

AGATHA
Then someone is paid to see the street dark.

ELIAS
Constable, it was a rough night. You may go.

JOLLIFFE
I'd rather wait, sir.

Elias stirs his tea. Agatha turns a page. Tom watches the stove. The rain does not let up.
